nation(G)\\n            diff = (r + 1) - rd\\n            if diff > 0:\\n                print(f'FOUND COUNTEREXAMPLE! n={n}, diff={diff}, edges={list(G.edges())}')\\n                found = True\\n                break\\n            # mutate: add or remove an edge\\n            u, v = random.sample(range(n), 2)\\n            if G.has_edge(u, v):\\n                if G.degree(u) > 1 and G.degree(v) > 1:\\n                    G.remove_edge(u, v)\\n                    if not nx.is_connected(G):\\n                        G.add_edge(u, v)\\n            else:\\n                G.add_edge(u, v)\\n        if found:\\n            break\\n    if found:\\n        break\\nif not found:\\n    print('No counterexample found by local search!')\\n\\\"\\nTask logs are available at: file:///tmp/agyhome.NWyWnj/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/92752dbc-e919-4b9e-b022-202726747cbe/.system_generated/tasks/task-73.log\\nYOU MUST TAKE ONE OF THE FOLLOWING TWO ACTIONS: A) either proceed to other relevant work (if any) or, B) simply update the user with a short message (that you have launched the command and will wait for it to finish) and end the turn.\\n DO NOTHING ELSE.\"}\n73: {\"step_index\":74,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T14:56:49Z\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"search_web\",\"args\":{\"query\":\"\\\"\\\\\\\"roman domination number\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"radius of a graph\\\\\\\"\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Searching the web\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Search literature for radius and roman domination\\\"\"}}]}\n74: {\"step_index\":75,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"GENERIC\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T14:56:51Z\",\"content\":\"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:56:51+02:00\\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:56:55+02:00\\nThe search for \\\"\\\"roman domination number\\\" \\\"radius of a graph\\\"\\\" returned the following summary:\\nThe **Roman domination number** ($\\\\gamma_R(G)$) and the **radius** of a graph ($\\\\text{rad}(G)$) are two distinct structural parameters in graph theory that are often studied together to understand the efficiency and reach of domination sets within a graph's topology[1][2][3].\\n\\n### Definitions\\n*   **Roman Domination Number ($\\\\gamma_R(G)$):** This is the minimum weight of a Roman dominating function, which is a labeling $f: V \\\\to \\\\{0, 1, 2\\\\}$ such that every vertex labeled $0$ is adjacent to at least one vertex labeled $2$[4][5]. It models scenarios where resources (legions) must be positioned to cover all vertices, where a \\\"2\\\" represents a vertex with a legion capable of defending itself and its neighbors, and \\\"1\\\" represents a vertex with a legion that can defend itself[6][7].\\n*   **Radius ($\\\\text{rad}(G)$):** The radius is defined as the minimum eccentricity among all vertices in the graph[4][8]. The eccentricity of a vertex $u$ is the maximum distance from $u$ to any other vertex in the graph[4][8].\\n\\n### Relationship and Research\\nResearch often explores how these parameters interact to provide insight into graph structure[2]. Key points regarding their relationship include:\\n\\n*   **Comparative Studies:** There is academic interest in comparing the Roman domination number with the radius, as well as other parameters like the total domination number and distance-2 domination number[1][3]. These studies aim to establish bounds or correlations that hold for specific classes of graphs (e.g., trees, trapezoid graphs, or grid graphs)[1][9][3].\\n*   **Structural Bounds:** Mathematicians often seek to establish bounds for domination parameters in terms of graph invariants like the radius, diameter, and maximum degree[10][11]. For instance, certain broadcast domination numbers have been shown to be determined by the minimum of the domination number and t\\n<truncated 2959 bytes>\\n-api-redirect/AUZIYQGAuC7cvdyYFn4QFl3zbP2U6qEAeDYXNVQpKSXndW41-kXDGrMJuWuePtBK4ns5LaGWx1tP60Yp1C3lRyGK30Ks9yhcrbJsfxryQUtyrP-TTbgu3